    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">How Cold Does It Get on a Sunset Boat Trip in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>17 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Not cold, but cooler than you expect. Evening air in Funchal usually sits between the mid-teens in winter and the low 20s in summer, and the boat&#x27;s speed, the sea air and the ride back after sunset make it feel cooler still, so bring a warm layer in every season.</p>

      <h2>What is the air temperature during a sunset trip?</h2>
<p>Somewhere between the day's high and the night's low, and falling as you go. The Chifbay <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> leaves 75 minutes ahead of sunset: you start in the last warm light and finish in dusk or full dark. The table shows roughly when it leaves in different months, next to the average daily high and low at the Funchal observatory from IPMA's 1991 to 2020 normals.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Month (mid-month)</th><th>Sunset, about</th><th>Trip leaves, about</th><th>Average high</th><th>Average low</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>December</td><td>18:05</td><td>16:50</td><td>21.0°C</td><td>15.5°C</td></tr>
<tr><td>March</td><td>19:15</td><td>18:00</td><td>20.6°C</td><td>14.3°C</td></tr>
<tr><td>June</td><td>21:15</td><td>20:00</td><td>23.7°C</td><td>18.2°C</td></tr>
<tr><td>August</td><td>20:55</td><td>19:40</td><td>26.8°C</td><td>20.7°C</td></tr>
<tr><td>October</td><td>19:35</td><td>18:20</td><td>25.2°C</td><td>19.2°C</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The times move a little every day and jump by an hour when the clocks change at the end of March and the end of October, so check the sunset for your own date. A rough rule: on the way out it feels close to the day's high, and by the time you step back onto the pontoon it is nearer the low.</p>
<h2>Why does it feel colder on the boat than on the promenade?</h2>
<p>Because the air is moving over you. In the evening on land you might sit in still air beside a wall that has soaked up sun all day. On a boat, the breeze you feel is the wind plus the boat's own speed, and moving air carries heat away from bare arms and legs much faster. Add a little salt spray and the damp of sea air, and 19°C on your phone can feel like a jumper night.</p>
<p>The sun matters too. In the first hour it still warms your face and back. When it drops below the horizon, that warmth goes at once, even if the thermometer hardly moves.</p>
<h2>When during the trip do you feel the chill?</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Leaving Marina do Funchal.</strong> The sun is still up and often warm. A T-shirt or a light top is enough for most people.</li>
<li><strong>Running west past Câmara de Lobos.</strong> The boat's speed adds a fresh breeze. This is the first moment a layer helps.</li>
<li><strong>The turn.</strong> On the 2h trip you turn at Cabo Girão, on the 2h30 at Ribeira Brava. The sun is low and the cliffs glow.</li>
<li><strong>Sunset and after.</strong> Bright twilight lasts only about half an hour after the sun goes down. The 2h trip is back about 45 minutes after sunset, the 2h30 about 1 h 15 after, so the ride home is in dusk and then darkness, with the lights of Funchal ahead. This is the coldest part.</li>
</ol>
<h2>What should you wear?</h2>
<p>Dress as if you were going to sit at a seafront bar after dark, then add one layer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>June to September:</strong> light clothes, plus a long-sleeved top or a thin jacket for the ride back.</li>
<li><strong>October, November, March to May:</strong> a jumper or fleece and a light windproof jacket. Long trousers are wise.</li>
<li><strong>December to February:</strong> a warm layer, a windproof jacket, long trousers and closed shoes. A scarf or hat takes up no room.</li>
<li><strong>All year:</strong> soft-soled shoes for the deck and a bag that does not mind a little spray.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep your hands free and warm, too. The Insta360 360° video and the photos taken with a real camera are part of the trip, so nobody has to hold a phone up in the wind for the whole ride. Nobody swims on this trip, so leave the swimsuit at the hotel. Food and drinks are included on every Chifbay trip, from local wine to poncha. A glass of poncha will make you feel warmer than you really are, so keep the jacket on anyway. For a full list, see <a href="/posts/what-to-pack-for-a-boat-trip-in-madeira">what to pack for a boat trip in Madeira</a>, and for the meeting point and timings, our <a href="/practical">practical page</a>.</p>
<h2>Which months give the warmest sunset trips?</h2>
<p>June to October. In those months the average night-time low at the Funchal observatory stays above 18°C, and in August and September it is above 20°C, so the ride back in the dark is pleasant in a light top. The sea is warmest then too, at about 23 to 24°C from August to October, which keeps the air over the water mild after sunset.</p>
<p>December to March are the coolest, with average lows of about 14 to 15.5°C. That is still mild by European standards, but it is the season when a T-shirt alone is not enough. In winter the sea, at about 18 to 20°C, is often warmer than the evening air above it, so the chill you feel comes from the moving air, not the water.</p>
<h2>Is the 2h or the 2h30 trip the better choice when it is cool?</h2>
<p>The 2h trip costs 400 EUR per boat and turns at Cabo Girão. The 2h30 costs 500 EUR per boat and goes on to Ribeira Brava, so you see more of the coast in the last light, with more time out after dark. On a warm summer evening the extra half hour is pure pleasure. On a cool winter evening, choose it only if everyone on board has a proper layer.</p>
<h2>Who will find it too cold?</h2>
<p>Hardly anyone in summer. In winter, it depends on you. If you feel the cold, or you travel with someone who does, choose the 2h trip, which turns at Cabo Girão and is back sooner after dark. Or swap the sunset for the 14:00 <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a>, which runs through the warmest hours and is back before a December sunset.</p>
<p>If you would like a blanket on board, ask us on WhatsApp before you book rather than assuming one will be there. And if you are weighing up a winter visit in general, our piece on whether <a href="/posts/are-boat-trips-in-madeira-worth-it-in-winter">boat trips in Madeira are worth it in winter</a> looks at the whole season, from sea temperature to lost days.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Do I need a jacket on a summer sunset trip in Madeira?</h3>
<p>A light one, yes. Even in August, when Funchal's evenings stay in the low 20s, the ride back after dark on a moving boat feels fresh on bare arms.</p>
<h3>Is the 2h30 Sunset Trip colder than the 2h one?</h3>
<p>Slightly. It turns further west at Ribeira Brava and comes back about 1 h 15 after sunset instead of about 45 minutes, so more of it is in the dark.</p>
<h3>Can you swim on a sunset boat trip?</h3>
<p>Not on ours. There is no swimming on the Sunset Trip. Swimming and paddle boards are part of the Day Trip.</p>
<h3>Are blankets provided on the boat?</h3>
<p>Blankets are not on our list of what is included, so ask us on WhatsApp before booking if you need one. Bringing your own warm layer is the safe choice.</p>

      <blockquote>Pack for the ride home, not for the ride out.</blockquote>
    </article>
